WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.249 --> 00:00:09.970
The Council recently held its March meeting,
and here’s a brief recap of some of the

00:00:09.970 --> 00:00:12.230
items discussed.

00:00:12.230 --> 00:00:15.850
Judge John Brandts-Giesen was presented with
a Community Service Award.

00:00:15.850 --> 00:00:21.089
It recognises the time and dedication he has
provided to various charities and organisations

00:00:21.089 --> 00:00:24.030
within the Waimakariri District and across
New Zealand.

00:00:24.030 --> 00:00:30.640
He was a solicitor in Rangiora for 22 years,
and was appointed as a Judge in 2016.

00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:36.500
The Council confirmed the name of the new
cycleway and walkway currently being built

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:38.260
between Rangiora and Woodend.

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:41.000
It will be known as the Rangiora-Woodend Path.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:45.360
It’s expected to be completed in middle
of the year, following the recent successful

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:50.000
opening of the Passchendaele Memorial Path
between Kaiapoi &amp; Rangiora.

00:00:51.239 --> 00:00:55.600
And following consultation with the community,
new speed limits were approved for Lees Road

00:00:55.640 --> 00:00:57.430
and Barkers Road in Kaiapoi.

00:00:57.430 --> 00:01:03.840
Both roads will now have a speed limit of
50km/h reduced from the current 80km/h.

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:08.000
Feedback from local residents was strongly
in favour of a reduction.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:14.400
The next monthly Council meeting will take
place on Tuesday 3rd April

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:16.240
at Council Chambers in Rangiora.

